From: Macron <noreply@github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] dxmt: hold a game to the frame rate a hot Mac can keep
 (dxmt.thermalPacing)

A Mac without a fan that a game keeps at full power is at thermal pressure
level 2 ("heavy") within a minute or two, and some tens of seconds later
macOS takes the power away itself. On a MacBook Air M3, Deadlock's practice
map ran at 70 frames a second while the Mac was cool, reached level 2 after
65 s and then ran at 14 to 30, with 70 to 90 ms from a frame's start to the
end of its GPU work and the game's sound mixer starved. Held to 45 frames a
second from the start (the game's own fps_max) it reached level 2 after
181 s and kept 45 for the minute that was left; held to 36 it did not
reach level 2 in four minutes; held to 60 it fell to 26.

So a game is given a limit when the level reaches 2, before macOS takes
more: about two thirds of what it was doing, a tenth lower every 20 s for
as long as the level stays, not below 24. At level 1 the limit goes up by
a twentieth every 20 s, but for five minutes not past nine tenths of the
rate the Mac last went to level 2 at; at level 0 by a tenth every 10 s. A
limit that is back at what the game ran at without one is removed. Nothing
is limited before level 2, so a Mac whose fan keeps it under that is never
held. ThermalPacer (dxmt_thermal.hpp) is that rule and nothing else;
Macron's repository runs it against scripted seconds and a model fitted
to the four starts above (runtime/dxmt/tests/thermal_pacer.cpp).

The limit is taken in the sleep of dxmt.latencySleep, at the end of
Present and before the next frame starts, so the game reads its input
after it and a held frame is no older when it is shown than a free one.
DXMT's own d3d11.preferredMaxFrameRate cannot do this: it waits for the
display, so on a 60 Hz panel 45 became 30.

winemetal.so reads the level (WMTGetThermalPressureLevel, unix call 133:
what `notifyutil -g com.apple.system.thermalpressurelevel` prints), once a
second. An info line says when a limit is set, changed or removed, and
dxmt.logLatency's line ends with the limit while there is one.

- dxmt.thermalPacing (default True).
---
